1. Scope
This license applies to original CVA-TTaempad software, documentation, templates
and branding supplied under this license (the Materials). Separately licensed
components retain their own terms. Written Permission means express permission
from TTaem specifying the recipient and authorized activities. Silence is not
permission.

2. Personal use and ordinary creator use
You may inspect, run and privately modify the Materials for personal use and
ordinary production of clips, video edits and creator videos. Publishing these
videos on monetized creator channels is permitted without separate project
permission. This covers using the tool in ordinary video-editing work; it does
not authorize offering the software itself as a paid or hosted service.
The rights and rules of the source broadcaster, platform and underlying video,
audio, chat, images and other content must still be respected.

3. Software distribution and services require Written Permission
Except for contribution forks under section 4, Written Permission is required
before distributing original or modified Materials as a separate software
product, package, installer, plugin, mirror or hosted service, even if free.
Selling or licensing the Materials, or operating a paid software or editing
service based on them, also requires Written Permission. An ordinary creator
publishing edited videos under section 2 is not operating such a software
service merely because their channel earns revenue. Sharing links to the
official project and sharing permitted output under section 5 are allowed.
Permission is limited to its named recipient, scope, duration and conditions;
transfer or sublicensing requires express authorization.

4. Contribution forks and contributions
You may publish proposed changes in a fork for contribution to the official
project. Keep this license, copyright and third-party notices; label the fork
as unofficial. It must not be offered as an independent product or service.
Platform terms remain effective. Before incorporation, contributors must have
authority over their work and explicitly agree to the contribution grant in
CONTRIBUTING.md. Opening a pull request alone does not transfer copyright.

5. Output and attribution
You may share videos, cut lists, timelines and summary pages generated by
permitted use, subject to rights in the underlying content. Sharing a summary
page includes only its necessary rendering assets, not an independent software
product or generation service. Generated HTML summary pages containing the
supplied TTaem CVA footer must keep its visible logo and readable ttaem.com link
to https://ttaem.com. If the logo cannot load, the text link must remain visible.
This requirement does not require adding a logo or watermark to exported MP4
videos or cut-list files. Attribution does not imply endorsement or accuracy.

6. Branding and content rights
Supplied branding may be used for attribution, official-project introductions
and contribution review. Representing a separate service as official or other
brand use requires Written Permission. TTaem does not acquire ownership of
your broadcasts, recordings, personal data or output through this license.
Project permission does not grant rights belonging to broadcasters, platforms
or other third parties.

7. Third parties and earlier grants
Third-party licenses, including those identified in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md,
remain effective. This license does not retrospectively revoke rights validly
granted under an earlier license. Previously MIT-licensed versions of the public
comment guide retain their MIT grants; the preserved notice is in
LICENSES/MIT-previous.txt in that repository. Copyright holders may offer
additional licenses for material they own or have authority to license.
This is a source-available license with restrictions, not an OSI-approved
open-source license. It does not authorize publication of private source.

8. Termination and warranty
Permissions terminate upon violation. Stop unauthorized activities and contact
TTaem for reinstatement. Third-party rights, earlier valid independent grants
and rights that cannot be excluded by law are unaffected.
